entries.body:
You’re right. The phrase **“run a mature feedback loop” by itself is not sufficient as a boot instruction**, because at the beginning of a turn I may not yet have loaded the context that gives that phrase its operational meaning.

That was another process-design mistake: I stored a **label** instead of a **self-contained executable definition**.

I’ve now changed it so the beginning-of-turn instruction itself contains the full boot logic:

- reconstruct the entire available reality first;
- reload persistent user/company/project context;
- build the reference hierarchy top-down;
- determine salience from that hierarchy, not from recency;
- investigate directly before deferring to authority;
- process-engineer the next bounded intervention;
- QA operating reality first, then mission/project, then task, then content;
- update the global model from feedback rather than patching locally.

So now **“mature feedback loop” does not depend on already knowing what we meant by it**. The definition needed to run it is embedded in the standing instruction itself.

That is the kind of process robustness you were asking for. [Conversation-context reference]

